## Premises and accepted domain

All original factors and states are retained. H=H0+K_X is supplied unchanged, including its scalar, with a certified full-space ||H||<=h0 and [H,N_X]=0. H need not commute with a clock occupation or either data Pauli. Its locality premise is reused from the fixed sources; H0 and its availability are not rederived from minimal axioms.

Added resources are fresh product-vacuum qubits, nearest-neighbor one-/two-qubit coefficient access including signed hoppings and both source quadratures, the displayed compensations, and the stated preparation/calibration/isolation/timing precision. Computable coefficients and a certified positive radicand margin are required. Rational positive q and a certified rational Lambda bound can be chosen; maximum-equality decisions are unnecessary.

The nominal theorem compares all outputs, including every new qubit and passive reference, at the same physical time, on arbitrary original states supported on N_X=1. It is not merely a projected-subspace or reduced-data comparison. For imperfect implementation, external errors must fit the requested tolerance; increasing Delta alone does not remove a fixed preparation, timing or isolation floor.

## Distinct response calculation and both odd counterterms

Use symmetric and antisymmetric core modes u=(r+a)/sqrt(2), v=(r-a)/sqrt(2). Set

    alpha=959/960, nu=961/960, p=sqrt(2)/8,
    delta=alpha-p^2=929/960.

The (u,b) block of G is [[alpha,p],[p,1]], and its v eigenvalue is nu. Thus the corresponding block of D_s is

    [[1/delta, -p/delta],
     [-p/delta, alpha/delta+s/4]],

with v eigenvalue 1/nu. Direct two-by-two inversion gives

    (D_s^(-1))_uu=(4 alpha+s delta)/(4+s)
                 =953/960 (s=+1), 969/960 (s=-1)
                 =nu-s/120,
    (D_s^(-1))_vv=nu.

Transforming back yields

    (D_s^(-1))_ra=-s/240,
    (D_s^(-1))_rr=(D_s^(-1))_aa=nu-s/240.

Also det G=nu delta=892769/921600. The same decomposition yields D_rr=D_aa=907200/892769, D_ra=15360/892769, D_rb=D_ab=-115320/892769 and D_bb=921599/892769. This checks the numerical constants and signs without assuming the author's rank-one response formula.

For an internal path, endpoint couplings 1 and -D_ij/g_arc give the Schur contribution +D_ij. The installed lower block D+Q^T T^(-1)Q therefore cancels the bare core chord when that arc is subdivided. Distinct arcs have disjoint internal supports, so they introduce no extra cross-compensation. Their diagonal shifts and the transport-root diagonal shift are essential. Direct arcs retain D_ij. The path/transport inverse entries used as denominators are strictly positive by the convergent walk series.

A second direct check completes the inverse source quadratic form. For a source (w_E,w_C), the block factorization gives

    w* B_s^(-1)w =
      w_E* T^(-1)w_E
      +(w_C-Q^T T^(-1)w_E)* D_s^(-1)
         (w_C-Q^T T^(-1)w_E).

On clock occupation j, w_E=z_j e_(u_j) and w_C=q Pc e_a. The negative transport coupling makes the effective core source beta_j z_j e_r+q Pc e_a. Its full contraction is

    gamma_j |z_j|^2
    +(c0-Pe/240)(beta_j^2 |z_j|^2+q^2)
    -(q beta_j Re z_j/120) Pc Pe.                   (R1)

This checks the complex conjugation, negative cross sign and both diagonal terms together. It remains valid for a Y data Pauli: Pc and Pe commute because their sites differ, and Y|0>=i|1> creates z, not its conjugate.

With Re(beta_j z_j)=120 f_j/q and beta_j^2|z_j|^2=Lambda^2, the cross term is -f_j Pc Pe and the two odd shifts sum to -(q^2+Lambda^2)Pe/240. Since n_j resolve the clock identity on N_X=1,

    J*VRVJ =
      c0 q^2 I
      +sum_j Lambda^2(gamma_j/beta_j^2+c0)n_j
      -(q^2+Lambda^2)Pe/240
      -sum_j f_j n_j Pc Pe.

This verifies C=C0-W and the installed negative Pe field, including its Pc-source square contribution. Neither odd term is optional. Unequal real transported squares leave a clock-dependent Pe shift; the second quadrature repairs that with constant transported modulus on the same physical neighbor. This is a failure of the real-source ansatz, not a no-go for one-port mediators.

On general clock inputs every n_j n_k remains in the quadratic form. Clock vacuum retains the artificial -Lambda^2 Pe/240 counterterm; multiple excitations retain extra cross-clock terms. The full-clock extension would be false for these counterterms. Exact N_X conservation of the nominal generator is load-bearing.

## Whole-qubit gap, kernel and noncommuting axes

The row sums give 3I/4<=G<=5I/4, hence D_s>=11I/20. T>=3I/4 and ||F^(-1)||<=1+(4/3)Qabs imply

    B_s>=gI,  g=(11/20)/[1+(4/3)Qabs]^2>0.

The entire compensated matrix is included; diagonal dominance of B_s or a common sign of hoppings is unnecessary.

For an arbitrary auxiliary state psi set phi_v=sigma_v^- psi. Then

    <psi|(L_s-gN_A)|psi>
      =sum_(u,v)(B_s-gI)_(uv)<phi_u|phi_v> >=0.

This is a positive matrix quadratic form on the direct sum of vectors phi_v. Hard-core qubit constraints do not invalidate it, and no free-particle approximation is used. Resolving Pe proves the joint inequality L>=gN_A. Its kernel is exactly the entire original space tensored with auxiliary vacuum, and ||R||<=1/g on all nonvacuum sectors. The proposed sum of positive diagonals, absolute hopping coefficients and h_d bounds ||L|| because each displayed block has norm one. The Pe-only part of h_d Pe n_b=(h_d/2)(Pe-Pe Z_b) remains physically installed with its partner.

For three components, L_i>=g_iN_i remains an operator inequality on the shared e factor. Adding gives

    L=sum_i L_i>=g_min sum_i N_i>=g_min Q0,

despite noncommuting Pe_i. Every N_i commutes with every L_k. On a sector with only component i excited, every other L_k vanishes because its auxiliaries are vacuum. The global inverse restricted there is exactly the i inverse. Cross-component second-order vacuum return vanishes by auxiliary occupation labels, not by commutation of data Paulis. The contractions therefore add to the stated global C0-W.

On sectors with two components excited the inverse is that of the actual sum with noncommuting Pe fields. It is not the sum or product of component inverses. The source uses that global R and its proven bound. The commuting target products X_cX_e,Y_cY_e,Z_cZ_e do not justify commuting the individual penalty detunings.

## Full residual, bare outputs and a mixed-sector defect witness

Write X=RVJ, Y=RVX and Z=J-epsilon X+epsilon^2Y, epsilon=Delta^(-1/2). R preserves auxiliary number and kills vacuum, so X has number one and Y number two, including different-component sectors. Directly,

    LX=VJ, LY=Q0VX, P0VX=J(C0-W).

Expanding K_Delta Z-ZH_tar cancels the sqrt(Delta) terms. Its constant terms are AJ+Q0VX-VX=AJ-J(C0-W)=JH_tar. The exact remainder is

    epsilon[XH_tar-AX+VY]
      +epsilon^2[AY-YH_tar].                        (R2)

VY retains both one- and three-excitation outputs. No H, C0, L, R or Pauli commutator is set to zero. This checks the full residual, not independent one-particle dynamics.

An analytic failure test makes the mixed correction consequential. Fix one clock occupation j, two axes i,k, and set all target f to zero. The allowed sources then satisfy z_i=i kappa_i,z_k=i kappa_k with positive amplitudes kappa_i=Lambda_i/beta_(ij),kappa_k=Lambda_k/beta_(kj). Denote

    D_i=(R_i)_(u_(ij),u_(ij)),
    C_i=(R_i)_(u_(ij),core a_i)=-beta_(ij)Pe_i/240.

Here D_i is strictly positive and C_i Pc_i is Hermitian. The coefficient of Q0VX at the auxiliary state with precisely both clock ports u_(ij),u_(kj) excited is

    M=z_i z_k(D_i+D_k)
      +z_k q_i C_i Pc_i+z_i q_k C_k Pc_k.

Its Hermitian part is

    (M+M*)/2=-kappa_i kappa_k(D_i+D_k)<0.

Thus the mixed sector is nonzero even when the target W vanishes. This test keeps the actual unchanged H, because Q0VX is independent of it. Deleting Y leaves the order-one residual -Q0VX. The candidate's global Y cancels exactly that defect. No many-qubit matrix or new numerical run was needed.

The global bounds ||X||<=x=v/g and ||Y||<=y=v^2/g^2 make (R2) at most epsilon[(a+b)x+vy]+epsilon^2(a+b)y. Duhamel between the full unitaries and two bare endpoint differences, each <=epsilon x+epsilon^2y, gives the source's F1/sqrt(Delta)+F2/Delta. Z need not be an isometry or a supplied dressed preparation. Tensoring passive references preserves operator norm; purification gives the complete-output trace bound min(2,2E_Delta). This establishes the finite-horizon, same-time, bare-vacuum statement with all auxiliary outputs retained.

## Uniform literal geometry and isolation

The inherited nonclock inventory lies at z=-1,0,1 and y<=2, with the specified transverse memory rays. Negative-x rays have (y,z) in {(0,0),(1,0),(0,1),(-1,0),(0,-1)}. The clock starts at X_*=max(n_old-1,Q_mem)+2>=5,Y_*=1-L_b<=-7 and lies at z=3. The exact ray coordinates, rather than just their bounding box, are needed near c and e.

I checked the described segments for all admitted L_b,Q_mem,n_old and integer q_c>=1. Cores 1 and 2 are four-cycles. Core 3 is the internally disjoint union of paths of lengths 5 and 11, giving 16 vertices; all three terminal arcs are subdivided. Its z=1 vertices avoid old positive-x z=1 rays because their y values are 1,3,4 rather than 0 or 2. Core 1 avoids the negative rays by transverse coordinates; core 2 lies at z=2.

| Component | Tail length | Transport cycle size | Fresh vertices |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| 1 | X_*+23-Y_* | M | M+X_*+27-Y_* |
| 2 | X_*+15-Y_* | M | M+X_*+19-Y_* |
| 3 | X_*+3-Y_* | M+8 | M+X_*+27-Y_* |

A tail starts at a new vertex and ends on its cycle, so its length is the number of added vertices beyond the cycle. Therefore m=3M+3X_*+73-3Y_* and |E_B,i|=m_i+1.

Tail 1 exits at x=-3 through y=3, descends to z=-2, and rises at y=Y_*-4. Tail 2 exits at x=-1,z=3 and uses z=5 before descending only to z=4. Tail 3 uses x=3,z=3, then y=Y_*-2 before reaching its outer-cycle corner. The first two cycles lie at z=2,4; the outer one has z=3 and x>=X_*-1>=4. These inequalities exclude cross-component and original-clock collisions, including X_*=5,q_c=1, and show each tail meets its cycle only at the designated endpoint. No upper bound on q_c is needed.

The outgoing-side convention gives distinct M outward clock ports and uses no outer-cycle corner as a source. Incoming-side adjacencies at clock corners remain isolation edges. Fresh declared degree is at most four. c uses exactly its three free neighbors (-1,1,1),(0,1,2),(1,1,1); e uses (-1,2,1),(0,2,2),(0,3,1). Each old clock site has two clock bonds and three declared new neighbors, hence degree five. No old factor is transit.

These are collision and declared-edge statements, not induced-graph statements. At X_*=5, for example, the third tail at x=3,z=3 is adjacent to the outer left side at x=4 where the y intervals overlap. The final E_iso correctly includes every omitted NN pair touching fresh factors, including same-component chords.

The general edge bound follows from incidence counting. Declared fresh–fresh edges number m+3 and consume 2m+6 fresh incidences. Declared fresh–old edges number 3M+6 and consume that many. At most 6m fresh incidences exist, so

    |E_iso|<=6m-(2m+6)-(3M+6)=4m-3M-12.

Every omitted edge consumes at least one remaining incidence; counting a fresh–fresh edge only once is conservative. The weaker 6m bound also holds. The selected count 35 is not used in this uniform proof.

For residual Hermitian edges E_uv, ||sum E_uv||<=zeta_iso=sum||E_uv|| must be a full-space bound after physical scaling, including Delta factors where applicable. Full-space Duhamel adds at most 2T_fin zeta_iso in trace norm even if errors break N_X or auxiliary-number conservation. An additional scalar reserve zeta_iso restores the sufficient positivity certificate and must enter scalar, mean, norm and comparator phase. One-particle error norms would not suffice. Onsite faults, original–original changes and additional occupied factors need their separately declared enlarged error inventory; the selected 35 cannot conceal them.

## Scalar, moments, resources and precision

The normalized full Pauli scalar is

    scalar(H)+(Delta/2)sum_i Tr B_i+S0+(1/2)sum_j S_j.

Pe detunings/counterterms and sources are globally traceless, whereas B diagonals and clock counterterms are not. In particular the odd Pe counterterm is not a scalar. Every subdivision/root compensation contributes to Tr B. No original H scalar is removed.

L>=0, S0+sum S_jn_j>=0, H>=-h0I, -sum d_iPe_i>=-Dsum I and sqrt(Delta)V>=-sqrt(Delta)v I justify the whole-space reserve h0+Dsum+sqrt(Delta)v. Dsum is conservative for the noncommuting Pe vector. The reserve changes actual scalar, mean, norm and comparator phase; full-space implementation error zeta needs an additional reserve zeta.

On bare vacuum, L annihilates both sides and V has zero first moment. Orthogonal flips give

    mean(K)=Tr rho(H+C0),
    Var(K)=Var_rho(H+C0)
      +Delta sum_i[q_i^2+sum_j |z_(ij)|^2 Tr(rho n_j)].

Different-axis flips occupy distinct fresh sites, so their cross terms vanish in this contraction despite noncommuting data Paulis. All H/C0 covariances remain. Reserves shift the mean but not variance. Auxiliary leakage <=E_Delta^2 gives only the penalty-energy bound Delta ell_L E_Delta^2, not energetic return or reuse.

The per-component count m_i+|E_B,i|+3M+4 is correct for the declared blocks: diagonals/hoppings, two real quadratures per clock port, M clock counterterms, Pc source, Pe detuning, s0 and Pe counterterm. The conservative three-component total is 2m+9M+15 before a reserve. Combining onsite coefficients may reduce physical count but cannot remove their sizes/errors. If a block is calibrated through independently imperfect Pauli terms, each such term must be included in the norm error.

The source edge load sqrt(Delta)|z_j|, or sqrt(Delta)(|Re z_j|+|Im z_j|) under separate quadrature charging, and the stated c/e, diagonal/hopping, clock-onsite, scalar and reserve loads account for all terms. beta_j can be extremely small; couplings, compensations, Delta, variance and calibration precision can be extremely costly. Positive denominators establish finite computable prices, not common caps or efficient scaling.

For the optional Hermitian sampled identity U=sum u_jn_j with real supplied u_j, adding sum|u_j| to a,b,full norm and reserve is sufficient. Its exact scalar contribution is sum u_j/2; moments use H+C0+U and M more coefficient errors are priced. A supplied sampled SWAP logarithm for this pair is covered. Full waveform manufacture and the complete preparation word are not.

A complete Hamiltonian error zeta adds at most 2T_fin zeta in trace norm; preparation trace error adds once; terminal timing costs at most 2(B_full+reserve)|delta t| with calibration integrated over the actual interval. These bounds require no number-conservation assumption for errors. The desired tolerance must be split between these external errors and E_Delta.

